When you type https://www.google.com into your browser and press enter, several processes occur behind the scenes.

Firstly, a DNS request translates www.google.com into an IP addresss so the browser knows which server to contact.

The browser then communicates with the server using TCP/IP. IP routes the data to the correct destination, while TCP provides reliable communication.

Firewall then checks network traffic and blocks connections that are not allowed.

Since the website uses https, TLS encrypts the comms between the browser and server, helping protect the data from being read or modified.

The request may then reach a load balancer, which distributes incoming traffic between multiple servers.

Web servers handle the http request and can serve static content or forward the request to the app server.

The app server then runs the website's backend logic. If info is needed, it will then request data from the database.

The response is then returned to the browser, which processes the content and displays the webpage.